S1: - joinpoint: a candidate point in the program’s execution where an aspect can be plugged in.
- A join point is a place in a program where an aspect may be included (woven).
- These are the points where your aspect’s code can be inserted into the normal flow of your application to add new behavior.
- Some examples of Joinpoints, this point could be: method call, method returning normally, method throwing an exception, exception being thrown, instantiating an object, a field being modified, referring an object, etc.

joinpoint: ● || S2: A join point is an event that occurs during the
execution of a program.

There are many possible types of event that
may occur during program execution.

So, it could be a method call, the initialization
of a variable, the updating of a field, etc. || S3: - A join point model defines the set of events that can be referenced in an aspect-oriented program.
- Join point models are not standardized and each aspect-oriented programming language has its own join point model.
- For example, in AspectJ events that are part of the join point model include: 1. Call events: Calls to a method or constructor. 2. Execution events: Execution of a method or constructor. 3. Initialization events: Class or object initialization. 4. Data events: Accessing or updating a field. 5. Exception events: The handling of an exception. || S4: [Figure] (page 16)
